 "sentences": [
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "text": "Chapter two. The Air Deccan deal, or how to buy an airline just to skip a queue.",
   "pauseAfter": 0.6
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "text": "By two thousand seven, Kingfisher Airlines was the shiny new toy of India's skies."
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "text": "Red planes, leather seats, free headphones, and a boss who loved a good party."
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "text": "Air Deccan was the opposite: India's first low cost airline, famous for tickets that cost one rupee."
  },
  {
   "id": "s5",
   "text": "So Vijay Mallya wrote a cheque for about five hundred and fifty crore rupees, for roughly a quarter of the company."
  },
  {
   "id": "s6",
   "text": "The papers loved it. The king of good times had just bought the people's airline."
  },
  {
   "id": "s7",
   "text": "But the real prize was hiding inside a very boring rule."
  },
  {
   "id": "s8",
   "text": "In India, an airline had to fly at home for five years before it could fly abroad."
  },
  {
   "id": "s9",
   "text": "Kingfisher had flown for two years. Air Deccan had flown for four.",
   "pauseAfter": 0.6
  },
  {
   "id": "s10",
   "text": "So the plan was simple: merge, borrow Deccan's age, and reach London faster."
  },
  {
   "id": "s11",
   "text": "Imagine marrying someone just because they already have a gym membership."
  },
  {
   "id": "s12",
   "text": "On paper, one airline sold luxury and the other sold bargains."
  },
  {
   "id": "s13",
   "text": "Under one roof, Deccan became Kingfisher Red, and the costs quietly began to pile up."
  },
  {
   "id": "s14",
   "text": "That deal would come back to haunt everyone involved."
  }
 ],
